{"attack": true, "case": "The target's claim and mechanism advance two mutually incompatible justifications, and the one named in the claim ('verisimilitude') smuggles in exactly the induction the target disclaims. Verisimilitude is closeness to truth; to call a theory's verisimilitude 'surviving' (or highest among survivors) is to assert that withstanding severe tests is truth-indicating — i.e., that past test-success raises the theory's standing on a truth-tracking metric. That is the inductive link the target's own countercondition 2 flags ('If verisimilitude cannot be defined without probabilistic assumptions, the defense collapses'). The mechanism concedes the point by quietly substituting a different criterion: it says we prefer the robust theory 'not because it is likely true, but because it is the best available target for future criticism and the most informative if false.' Informativeness-if-false is orthogonal to verisimilitude: a wildly false but bold theory can be maximally informative and an excellent criticism target while having near-zero verisimilitude. So either (a) the 'wager on verisimilitude' framing in the claim relies on an inductive inference from surviving tests to closeness to truth, contradicting the non-inductive posture, or (b) the mechanism's actual rule (informativeness) is not a wager on verisimilitude at all and the claim's stated rationale is misdescribed. The defense is therefore not 'defensible on Popper's terms' as stated; it equivocates between a truth-tracking metric that requires induction and a non-truth-tracking utility (informativeness) that does not justify the 'verisimilitude' label.", "premise": "A 'wager on the theory with the highest surviving verisimilitude' is only a rational (non-arbitrary) wager if withstanding severe tests is a non-inductive indicator of closeness to truth; but the target's mechanism explicitly denies that test-survival bears on the theory's probability/truth, replacing the justification with 'most informative if false,' which is a distinct criterion that does not track verisimilitude.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "2650be204412ca66016f0b7babe92cd06b819f24694f454d8345f6a015551187", "quote": "it costs the agent any claim to probable truth, accepting instead that the choice is a wager on the theory with the highest surviving verisimilitude."}, {"block": "2650be204412ca66016f0b7babe92cd06b819f24694f454d8345f6a015551187", "quote": "we prefer the theory that has proven itself most robust against error, not because it is likely true, but because it is the best available target for future criticism and the most informative if false."}], "successor_question": "Can 'verisimilitude' be defined as a non-inductive, non-probabilistic function of a theory's test history such that surviving severe falsification attempts monotonically increases it without any assumption that past success is evidence of closeness to truth?"}